From: tezuka_fw
Subject: [PATCH] wg-quick: retry ip address add against a real-hardware race

The "ip address add" call that runs right after "wg setconf" in
cmd_up() can lose a race against the newly-configured WireGuard
interface settling in the kernel's routing/FIB state on some
platforms. Observed reliably on a Zynq-7010 ARM SoC (PlutoPlus):
"RTNETLINK answers: Network is unreachable" on every automated
wg-quick run, while the identical ip/wg commands succeed when spaced
apart manually. Retrying the whole wg-quick invocation does not help
(each attempt reproduces the same zero-delay sequence internally), and
a single fixed delay isn't reliable either — the race window is not a
fixed duration (confirmed: a 1s delay inserted at this exact point
fixed it on one test run but not on a later one). Retry the address
add itself instead, up to 5 times with a 1s pause between attempts, so
this adapts to however long the interface actually needs rather than
guessing a duration that has to always be long enough.

See github.com/mbehn1976/tezuka_fw pull/4 and pull/5 for the
on-hardware diagnosis this patch is based on.

--- a/src/wg-quick/linux.bash
+++ b/src/wg-quick/linux.bash
@@ -119,7 +119,19 @@
 add_addr() {
 	local proto=-4
 	[[ $1 == *:* ]] && proto=-6
-	cmd ip $proto address add "$1" dev "$INTERFACE"
+	# On some platforms (observed reliably on a Zynq-7010 ARM SoC) this
+	# can lose a race against the newly-configured WireGuard interface
+	# settling in the kernel's routing/FIB state right after set_config,
+	# failing with "RTNETLINK answers: Network is unreachable" — and the
+	# race window isn't a fixed duration, so retry rather than guess a
+	# single delay long enough to always cover it. See
+	# github.com/mbehn1976/tezuka_fw pull/4 and pull/5.
+	local n
+	for n in 1 2 3 4 5; do
+		cmd ip $proto address add "$1" dev "$INTERFACE" && return
+		[[ $n -lt 5 ]] && sleep 1
+	done
+	return 1
 }

 set_mtu_up() {
